  "answer": "A UDP Flood attack works by an adversary sending a large volume of UDP packets to a target service, aiming to consume the available network bandwidth and deny legitimate users access to that service. This type of attack can overwhelm not only the target's network resources but also the resources of firewalls, which may open a port for each incoming UDP connection, thus saving connection states. The high packet rate characteristic of UDP floods can lead to resource exhaustion on both the target and intermediary devices like firewalls. Additionally, UDP Flood attacks can target specific services such as DNS or VoIP, further disrupting their functionality [Source 1][Source 3].",
